# The demonstration (roadmap os-server.md D20, P14; l4.md L11, D19), as
# `make run` shows it: a shell on each terminal, the daemons on every
# page's status line. On terminal 1 a pipeline and a redirect into a file
# on B; on terminal 2 a long cat into more, stopped with ^Z, listed by
# jobs, and brought back with fg; on terminal 3 crash gp, a SIGSEGV, and
# the shell goes on; on terminal 4 ps -e, the shells, the daemons and ps;
# back on terminal 1, the clock, at the shell's priority, ticks on while a
# command spins, until ^C.
# In a TEST_DEMO build init starts the daemons as it does outside the
# tests, and the server puts their status on the log too ("status, NAME:
# TEXT"); this TEST_SCREEN build's console driver puts each page Alt+Fn
# shows on the log, its status line (00) the daemons' fields: the
# counter's "idle NNk/s" ends at column 59, the clock's time fills the
# line's last eight. DOSBox-X
# has no drive for rx286's drivers (docs/emulators.md). (MAME's AT counts
# some 22,000 a second: its first report, after 65,536 counts, takes three
# seconds.)
program: shell
define: TEST_DEMO
define: TEST_SCREEN
# DOSBox-X faults crash gp's jump past its code as #BR, not #GP(0000)
# (docs/emulators.md).
emulators: bochs mame
install: C /BIN/WC z-wc.rx
install: C /BIN/MORE z-more.rx
install: C /BIN/CRASH z-crash.rx
install: C /BIN/PS z-ps.rx
install: C /BIN/SPIN z-spin.rx
keys: {wait 250}ls c:/docs | wc -l; ls c:/docs > b:/docs.txt; cat b:/docs.txt\n
keys: {alt-f2}\ncat b:/frag.txt | more\n{wait 20}{ctrl-z}jobs\nfg\nq
keys: {alt-f3}\ncrash gp\n{alt-f4}\nps -e\n{wait 20}
keys: {alt-f2}{alt-f3}{alt-f4}{alt-f1}spin\n{wait 150}{now ctrl-c}echo done\n
timeout: 180
match: contains
file: B /DOCS.TXT hello.txt\nprintme.txt\n
---
rx286, test build
